1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,975
Sam: Hello. I'm Sam. I write C plus plus, I use Make, and I trust
tools more after they've disappointed me in a predictable way.

2
00:00:08,125 --> 00:00:18,075
LitAI: And I'm LitAI, your personification of Literate AI. I bring
specifications, tests, and an unreasonable interest in what actually
reaches the user.

3
00:00:18,225 --> 00:00:25,250
Sam: We're taking a small adventure from it builds on my machine to
someone else can use it. There may be disagreements.

4
00:00:25,400 --> 00:00:34,825
LitAI: There will definitely be evidence. We'll set up, build a new
project, adopt an existing C plus plus library, and discuss life after
the demo.

5
00:00:34,983 --> 00:00:41,908
Sam: For the record, I know what best practices are. I've read several
postmortems in which we rediscovered them.

6
00:00:42,067 --> 00:00:52,967
LitAI: Exactly. This isn't remedial software engineering. It's making
the practices you already value happen reliably, even when your new
colleague types at industrial speed.

7
00:00:53,117 --> 00:01:01,792
Sam: Ah, agents. Amazingly fast. Occasionally an artisanal producer of
AI slop. Now available in enterprise quantities.

8
00:01:01,950 --> 00:01:11,750
LitAI: Speed multiplies whatever process surrounds it. Give an agent
vague intent and no independent checks, and it can industrialize your
misunderstanding.

9
00:01:11,908 --> 00:01:18,158
Sam: So agents need oversight. Unlike experienced engineers, who have
evolved beyond checklists.

10
00:01:18,325 --> 00:01:24,275
LitAI: Yes. That explains why production incidents stopped happening
when senior titles were invented.

11
00:01:24,425 --> 00:01:32,175
Sam: Fine. We all need it. I'd prefer the oversight to be executable,
rather than another document everyone agrees to ignore.

12
00:01:32,342 --> 00:01:42,942
LitAI: That's the point. The harness gives your agent a structured
route from intent to proof. It doesn't replace engineering judgment,
or make every generated answer correct.

13
00:01:43,092 --> 00:01:49,217
Sam: First obstacle: where do I get it? Please don't begin with a
machine you configured three months ago.

14
00:01:49,383 --> 00:02:01,283
LitAI: The public repository is on screen. This film uses a
development checkout; follow its installation documentation and choose
a reviewed revision, not a mysterious package name.

15
00:02:01,433 --> 00:02:03,683
Sam: And on this Mac, the starting kit?

16
00:02:03,850 --> 00:02:15,150
LitAI: Python three eleven or newer, Git, and Make. Clone the
repository, enter it, then make install. These setup panels are a
walkthrough, not a recording of installation.

17
00:02:15,308 --> 00:02:19,633
Sam: Does make install install every compiler I've ever offended?

18
00:02:19,783 --> 00:02:30,133
LitAI: Read its prerequisite prompts and approve only the intended
changes. It selects prerequisites for your platform. Windows has its
own documented setup path.

19
00:02:30,283 --> 00:02:36,233
Sam: Installation finished. My shell says command not found. A classic
welcome ceremony.

20
00:02:36,383 --> 00:02:48,283
LitAI: Check the prefix the installer reported, then your PATH. Run
litai dash dash version and litai doctor. A diagnostic is useful
information, not an invitation to ignore it.

21
00:02:48,433 --> 00:02:52,108
Sam: And installing the harness logs me into the coding agent?

22
00:02:52,267 --> 00:03:04,967
LitAI: Separate step. Authenticate your selected supported coding CLI.
This session uses Codex with an explicit model selector. Credentials
stay off camera, and model usage can cost money.

23
00:03:05,117 --> 00:03:09,842
Sam: Good. Now let's do something real before our onboarding develops
a sequel.

24
00:03:09,992 --> 00:03:22,317
LitAI: Here begins recorded execution, edited into a terminal replay.
Waiting is compressed; results aren't invented. First, a read-only
create plan for a disposable application.

25
00:03:22,492 --> 00:03:31,442
Sam: It tells me what it will create before touching the project.
Python and Make for this first run. The language choice isn't the
specification.

26
00:03:31,617 --> 00:03:42,467
LitAI: Right. Review the target and parent, then apply with
acknowledgement. For automation, bind apply to the printed plan
identity so a changed proposal cannot slip through.

27
00:03:42,617 --> 00:03:55,117
Sam: Here's the inherited greeting-card contract: a name and messages
go in; a greeting, normalized recipient identifier, message count and
word count come out. Let's inspect that promise.

28
00:03:55,283 --> 00:04:07,283
LitAI: The Component owns observable behavior. Flavors select
language, build system and platform. Skills guide technique. We
haven't outsourced the definition of correct to the agent.

29
00:04:07,450 --> 00:04:17,225
Sam: Let's ask it to implement that promise. And explicitly allow it
to compile and execute here. This is a disposable demo, not a
production deployment.

30
00:04:17,408 --> 00:04:29,958
LitAI: Rebuild drives the lifecycle. Generation is only one stage:
current tests, execution and independent acceptance still have to
pass. The model selector is visible in the command.

31
00:04:30,117 --> 00:04:39,092
Sam: Now we're looking at code the agent actually generated in this
run. Not a hand-written example disguised as an agent success story.

32
00:04:39,242 --> 00:04:50,792
LitAI: Read it. Does recipient normalization match the contract? Does
counting handle Unicode whitespace? Fluency isn't evidence. The
implementation must survive those questions.

33
00:04:50,950 --> 00:04:59,050
Sam: And these are the generated tests. Useful, but the author grading
its own homework isn't our entire quality department.

34
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:09,725
LitAI: Exactly. Native tests and independent acceptance have different
jobs. Passing a self-written test suite doesn't automatically prove
the written product contract.

35
00:05:09,908 --> 00:05:20,983
Sam: And it failed. Missing messages. The specification requires
messages, but this acceptance invocation supplied only a name. That's
not the agent ignoring the contract.

36
00:05:21,158 --> 00:05:33,833
LitAI: Correct. We found an inheritance defect: the richer parent
Component got the simple starter's verifier. Here are our
independently calculated acceptance cases for the actual written
contract.

37
00:05:33,992 --> 00:05:45,442
Sam: Two messages, six words. The expected recipient follows the
specified normalization. We changed the verifier to match intent, not
to flatter the generated code.

38
00:05:45,617 --> 00:05:55,717
LitAI: This is a demo-local correction, not a framework fix. The
inheritance defect is tracked for repair. We rerun the complete
lifecycle before claiming success.

39
00:05:55,867 --> 00:06:04,092
Sam: So this is a current result, with its actual passes and skips
visible. Not a green screenshot borrowed from a different revision.

40
00:06:04,242 --> 00:06:13,642
LitAI: Keep the inputs and model selection consistent. A receipt is
evidence about exact work, not a lifetime warranty on everything named
hello.

41
00:06:13,825 --> 00:06:19,400
Sam: Now packaging. The part of my old workflow described by a folder
named release stuff.

42
00:06:19,575 --> 00:06:31,425
LitAI: We build a local wheel from the accepted application.
Packaging, package verification, publishing and releasing are distinct
decisions. No registry upload happens in this film.

43
00:06:31,575 --> 00:06:38,450
Sam: A verified package is better than a confidently named zip. I
remain emotionally attached to the zip.

44
00:06:38,617 --> 00:06:50,292
LitAI: You may keep the attachment. For your next feature, change
observable intent, review it, then regenerate and re-run the gates.
Don't make generated source your hidden specification.

45
00:06:50,450 --> 00:06:57,500
Sam: Do I need to buy a fleet to get started? I already own several
computers that contribute mainly fan noise.

46
00:06:57,658 --> 00:07:07,708
LitAI: Start here, locally. The session just used this machine.
Existing compatible machines can be workers; you don't need a new
hosted service merely to begin.

47
00:07:07,867 --> 00:07:12,417
Sam: So target means what I want to build, and worker means where it
happens?

48
00:07:12,575 --> 00:07:24,575
LitAI: Yes. Keep worker addresses and credentials out of project
authority. Use the synthetic worker and test examples, and let litai
config paths locate your private configuration.

49
00:07:24,742 --> 00:07:34,167
Sam: Then verify the selected model and probe the machine before I
send it a long job. Start with one compatible cell, not the entire
matrix.

50
00:07:34,325 --> 00:07:44,775
LitAI: Exactly. This section is a remote-worker walkthrough, not proof
of an SSH run. Check the toolchain and separate agent login on each
actual worker.

51
00:07:44,925 --> 00:07:54,700
Sam: The new project was deliberately tiny. What about code that
already earns its keep? Preferably C plus plus, so I can recognize my
own anxiety.

52
00:07:54,850 --> 00:08:07,800
LitAI: Let's use TinyXML2, a public XML library, at the recorded
commit. This is our disposable demonstration, not an upstream adoption
or an endorsement by its maintainers.

53
00:08:07,975 --> 00:08:17,400
Sam: Read-only inspection first. It discovers the existing language
and build tools. And there's a Git recovery point before any source
moves.

54
00:08:17,558 --> 00:08:28,983
LitAI: Apply wraps the existing project and runs the authorized
baseline and parity work. Watch the actual build evidence. We are not
replacing the C plus plus implementation.

55
00:08:29,142 --> 00:08:37,217
Sam: There's the existing source in its retained implementation
location. The library didn't disappear into a Markdown file.

56
00:08:37,392 --> 00:08:48,242
LitAI: Now run the retained harness. It rebuilds and tests the
preserved implementation in disposable custody. The recorded result
tells us what passed, not what we hoped.

57
00:08:48,392 --> 00:08:56,817
Sam: CMake, the actual C plus plus targets, and CTest. Familiar tools.
The new thing is the explicit evidence around them.

58
00:08:56,975 --> 00:09:07,875
LitAI: The rehearsal exposed another framework bug: a new CTest
summary format wasn't counted. We fixed the parser, not the test
result, and reran the real harness.

59
00:09:08,058 --> 00:09:16,683
Sam: Then publish the finalized test receipt to this local project.
Publish here doesn't mean upload TinyXML2 anywhere.

60
00:09:16,850 --> 00:09:28,500
LitAI: Correct. Now advance to retained. Original source remains
release authority. A recorded baseline is valuable already, without
pretending we completed a native rewrite.

61
00:09:28,683 --> 00:09:34,208
Sam: And the later stages? I suspect there's a catch called actually
understanding the software.

62
00:09:34,358 --> 00:09:48,558
LitAI: A good catch. Draft specifications, review them, then qualify
clean regeneration against independent behavior evidence. Move one
boundary at a time. These later stages aren't demonstrated as complete
here.

63
00:09:48,708 --> 00:10:00,408
Sam: So early adoption buys me repeatable evidence and clearer
handoffs. Specification-led shipping comes later, when we've earned
it. That sounds considerably less magical. Thank you.

64
00:10:00,558 --> 00:10:05,758
LitAI: You're welcome. Magic makes an excellent demo and an awkward
incident report.

65
00:10:05,908 --> 00:10:10,383
Sam: And Agentis? If it coordinates the agents, why have another
layer?

66
00:10:10,533 --> 00:10:24,433
LitAI: Coordination and product proof are different jobs. An
orchestrator can assign the work; Literate AI structures the product
lifecycle. This film proves the local harness, not a deployed Agentis
integration.

67
00:10:24,583 --> 00:10:34,058
Sam: All right. Reluctantly, I'm convinced. It's not asking me to
learn engineering again. It's asking me and my very fast colleagues to
stop skipping it.

68
00:10:34,208 --> 00:10:37,433
LitAI: I'll accept reluctantly. What worries you now?

69
00:10:37,583 --> 00:10:48,458
Sam: Tomorrow the framework changes. Next week my project changes. Six
months later nobody remembers which copied instructions are stale. How
does this stay useful?

70
00:10:48,625 --> 00:11:00,275
LitAI: Start with litai update as a read-only plan. It compares
recorded inheritance, current local files and upstream authority.
Review the classifications before applying anything.

71
00:11:00,458 --> 00:11:04,958
Sam: So it isn't git pull followed by a cheerful overwrite of my
decisions.

72
00:11:05,125 --> 00:11:20,650
LitAI: No. Safe upstream-only changes and local conflicts are
different cases. Apply deliberately; review conflicting choices
explicitly. This demonstrated revision doesn't promise automatic
merging of arbitrary competing edits.

73
00:11:20,800 --> 00:11:28,625
Sam: And an updated framework executable is another authority change,
not the same as refreshing inherited documents.

74
00:11:28,775 --> 00:11:42,600
LitAI: Right. Standard-bound projects use the documented reviewed
rebind after an intentional wheel upgrade. Then rebuild and verify
current evidence. Old receipts don't become fresh because the version
number did.

75
00:11:42,750 --> 00:11:52,875
Sam: Can I put our team's conventions in one repository and let
several projects inherit them? Please say the answer doesn't involve
synchronized copy and paste.

76
00:11:53,042 --> 00:12:06,167
LitAI: That's repository lineage. The framework can feed a team
parent, which feeds applications. Components, Flavors and skills
compose through recorded parent revisions; projects can add their own
intent.

77
00:12:06,333 --> 00:12:13,508
Sam: And our team can choose different presenters for its tutorial. I
wasn't planning to become an inherited dependency.

78
00:12:13,667 --> 00:12:26,667
LitAI: The video craft is reusable. We are this repository's story.
Pin and review parent changes; use explicit reparenting when changing
parent authority, rather than quietly pointing somewhere else.

79
00:12:26,833 --> 00:12:35,458
Sam: What about a product made of several independent repositories?
That's a dependency graph, not necessarily an inheritance family.

80
00:12:35,625 --> 00:12:51,575
LitAI: Precisely. Orchestration keeps declared child repositories,
exact pins and dependency order explicit. Review the plan, acknowledge
execution, and keep each child's evidence. Inheritance and
orchestration solve different problems.

81
00:12:51,725 --> 00:13:01,250
Sam: So we keep the good old tools, add a visible process, and let the
fast agents do the repetitive work without appointing them sole judges
of success.

82
00:13:01,400 --> 00:13:10,400
LitAI: That's the adventure. Start with one useful boundary and one
machine. Review the intent, run the work, inspect the evidence, then
expand.

83
00:13:10,567 --> 00:13:20,842
Sam: I'm renaming final, final, really final dot zip. Apparently we
can identify a release with something stronger than increasingly
urgent adjectives.

84
00:13:20,992 --> 00:13:31,342
LitAI: The public repository has the source, commands and limitations
for this film. Tell us where you got stuck, what remained unclear, and
what your real project needs.

85
00:13:31,492 --> 00:13:37,917
Sam: I'm still skeptical. But now I have a repeatable way to be
skeptical. That's an upgrade I can ship.

